{% doc %}
  Renders the theme's import map and module scripts.

  Bare specifiers (`@theme/component`) are resolved by the browser's import
  map rather than by a bundler — the same approach Shopify's Horizon theme
  uses. This is what lets the theme ship plain ES modules with no build step
  while still writing readable imports.

  Because modules resolve at runtime, load order does not matter: a module
  can import another regardless of which `<script>` tag came first.

  Keep this list in step with the `paths` mapping in tsconfig.json, which is
  how TypeScript resolves the same specifiers for typechecking.
{% enddoc %}

<script type="importmap">
  {
    "imports": {
      "@theme/component": {{ 'component.js' | asset_url | json }},
      "@theme/media-gallery": {{ 'media-gallery.js' | asset_url | json }},
      "@theme/cart-api": {{ 'cart-api.js' | asset_url | json }},
      "@theme/cart-drawer": {{ 'cart-drawer.js' | asset_url | json }},
      "@theme/cart-count": {{ 'cart-count.js' | asset_url | json }},
      "@theme/product-form": {{ 'product-form.js' | asset_url | json }}
    }
  }
</script>

{% comment %}
  One entry module, importing through the SAME bare specifiers the import map
  declares. Components self-register, so importing is enough.

  ⚠️ Do not go back to `<script type="module" src="{{ 'x.js' | asset_url }}">`
  per component. `asset_url` inside a `src` attribute and `asset_url` inside the
  import map JSON do not always produce the same string — under
  `shopify theme dev` the tag is rewritten to the local proxy
  (`http://127.0.0.1:9292/cdn/...`) while the import map keeps the
  protocol-relative CDN host (`//shop.myshopify.com/cdn/...`).

  Two URLs means the browser evaluates the module twice, and the second
  `customElements.define()` throws:

    NotSupportedError: the name "cart-drawer" has already been used with this
    registry

  That error aborts whichever module triggered the second evaluation, so the
  visible symptom is unrelated components silently failing to upgrade — with
  nothing in the console pointing at the cause.

  Importing everything through the import map gives each module exactly one
  resolved URL, which is what the import map is for.
{% endcomment %}
<script type="module">
  import "@theme/media-gallery";
  import "@theme/cart-drawer";
  import "@theme/cart-count";
  import "@theme/product-form";
</script>
